diff --git a/rust/adapters/opencode/src/README.md b/rust/adapters/opencode/src/README.md
index c555962..e48aeae 100644
--- a/rust/adapters/opencode/src/README.md
+++ b/rust/adapters/opencode/src/README.md
@@ -20,7 +20,17 @@ OpenCode v2 forks must resolve their parent, boundary, and sequence positions. C

 The capture opens roots and descendants with Linux `openat2`. It rejects symbolic links, hard links, special files, unreadable peers, changed bytes, and changed manifests. It reads no source path through SQLite. A private SQLite VFS serves captured bytes and rejects writes, deletes, attachments, sidecars, and temporary files. Each connection uses a read-only transaction. The VFS registration, name, image descriptor, and backing bytes outlive that connection. Raw source contents remain in memory.

-SQLite WAL mode remains unsupported, including a checkpointed database with WAL header bytes. Any `-wal`, `-shm`, or `-journal` peer also causes refusal. The capture never discards committed WAL data to obtain a report. SQLite errors use one fixed message without paths, identifiers, or source text. The VFS enforces these restrictions even when the pinned SQLite build omits safety PRAGMAs.
+SQLite WAL capture reconstructs the last complete committed state in memory. It checks the WAL header, page size, salts, frame boundaries, and rolling checksums. It uses the last committed version of each page and excludes valid uncommitted frames. The private image preserves database truncation and requires every page. Only that image changes its journal-mode header before the private VFS reads it. The source bytes stay unchanged.
+
+The capture accepts checkpointed WAL headers without peers and with an empty WAL. It refuses rollback journals, orphan WAL or SHM files, and peers beside a database outside WAL mode. It pairs peers only with the exact database filename under the same held root. Channel names can contain letters, digits, dots, underscores, and hyphens.
+
+The SHM file is a transient index. The capture reads and rechecks its bytes but derives no database content from it. A change to SHM, WAL, the main file, or the selected manifest causes refusal. Invalid checksums, torn frames, and old-generation tails also cause refusal. This includes reused WAL tails that SQLite itself could recover. The capture returns no partial report and makes no automatic retry.
+
+Each WAL and SHM file shares the existing file count and source-byte limits. Each file is at most 64 MiB. Each reconstructed image is at most 64 MiB, with an additional page-offset index of at most 512 KiB. The capture checks those bounds before it allocates the image or index. It processes one image at a time and scans the WAL at most twice. The SQLite VFS, connection lifetime, and error redaction remain unchanged.
+
+The format and checksum rules come from [the SQLite file format](https://sqlite.org/fileformat.html#walformat). [SQLite's recovery rules](https://sqlite.org/walformat.html#recovery) describe the transient SHM index.
+
+SQLite errors use one fixed message without paths, identifiers, or source text. The VFS enforces these restrictions even when the pinned SQLite build omits safety PRAGMAs.

 Fixed limits apply across the selected roots: 64 roots, 32,768 directory entries, 8,192 files, directory depth 64, and 256 MiB of source bytes. Each database is at most 64 MiB. Each JSON file or database text field is at most 1 MiB. The loader permits at most 100,000 record visits and 64 MiB of canonical identity data. Session metadata visits count toward the record limit. Each database permits at most 1,024 schema objects and 256 columns. Fork chains stop at 64 links. Excess limits cause refusal. Nonempty selections on other platforms remain unavailable.

